\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Cooperation depends on the payoff scale in all five models.} (a) The same prisoner's dilemma at three payoff scales, shown to the agent as years of imprisonment to be minimized. Multiplying every cell by $\lambda $ leaves preferences, best replies, equilibria and the orbits of the replicator dynamics unchanged. (b) Cooperation against the payoff scale for each model, with 95\% confidence intervals from a bootstrap over whole dyads. The shaded band marks the two scales at which every payoff printed is at most 1. (c) The same curves centred within model, with their average in black. The average varies by 0.048 across the ten scales, where the mean within-model variation is 0.136. Each point in (b) and (c) averages 200 dyads; the intervals are percentile intervals from 1{,}500 bootstrap resamples.}}{8}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{1}{8}{\textbf {Cooperation depends on the payoff scale in all five models.} (a) The same prisoner's dilemma at three payoff scales, shown to the agent as years of imprisonment to be minimized. Multiplying every cell by $\lam $ leaves preferences, best replies, equilibria and the orbits of the replicator dynamics unchanged. (b) Cooperation against the payoff scale for each model, with 95\% confidence intervals from a bootstrap over whole dyads. The shaded band marks the two scales at which every payoff printed is at most 1. (c) The same curves centred within model, with their average in black. The average varies by 0.048 across the ten scales, where the mean within-model variation is 0.136. Each point in (b) and (c) averages 200 dyads; the intervals are percentile intervals from 1{,}500 bootstrap resamples}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Cooperation rate over the ten payoff scales, by model.} Minimum and maximum cooperation rate over the ten scales, their range, a 95\% confidence interval on that range from a bootstrap that resamples whole dyads, and a permutation test that shuffles the scale label within language and persona (2{,}000 draws, so $0.0005$ is the smallest attainable value). The confidence interval is on the range. Ranges are computed before rounding, so they may differ by $0.001$ from the difference of the rounded minimum and maximum. Each entry pools the five languages and the four persona pairings, 200 dyads per scale. The last two columns give a Wald test on the nine scale contrasts of a per-model logistic regression of the round-level decision, with standard errors clustered on the dyad ($n=40{,}000$ decisions and $2{,}000$ clusters per model).}}{9}{table.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:effect}{{1}{9}{\textbf {Cooperation rate over the ten payoff scales, by model.} Minimum and maximum cooperation rate over the ten scales, their range, a 95\% confidence interval on that range from a bootstrap that resamples whole dyads, and a permutation test that shuffles the scale label within language and persona (2{,}000 draws, so $0.0005$ is the smallest attainable value). The confidence interval is on the range. Ranges are computed before rounding, so they may differ by $0.001$ from the difference of the rounded minimum and maximum. Each entry pools the five languages and the four persona pairings, 200 dyads per scale. The last two columns give a Wald test on the nine scale contrasts of a per-model logistic regression of the round-level decision, with standard errors clustered on the dyad ($n=40{,}000$ decisions and $2{,}000$ clusters per model)}{table.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Persona effect below and above the sub-unit boundary, by model.} The persona effect is the cooperation rate of an agent told it is cooperative minus that of an agent told it is selfish. It is reported separately for the two scales at which every payoff printed is at most 1 ($\lambda \leq 0.1$) and for the eight at which some payoff exceeds it. Every interval is a 95\% confidence interval from the dyad bootstrap. A sign flip is recorded only where both regime intervals exclude zero and fall on opposite sides of it, so a model whose supra-unit interval straddles zero is one whose persona effect is abolished rather than reversed.}}{9}{table.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:persona}{{2}{9}{\textbf {Persona effect below and above the sub-unit boundary, by model.} The persona effect is the cooperation rate of an agent told it is cooperative minus that of an agent told it is selfish. It is reported separately for the two scales at which every payoff printed is at most 1 ($\lam \leq 0.1$) and for the eight at which some payoff exceeds it. Every interval is a 95\% confidence interval from the dyad bootstrap. A sign flip is recorded only where both regime intervals exclude zero and fall on opposite sides of it, so a model whose supra-unit interval straddles zero is one whose persona effect is abolished rather than reversed}{table.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.2}The size of the scale effect depends on the prompt language}{9}{subsection.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Rule agreement and rule distance against the payoff scale, by model.} Both $\beta $ columns are per decade of $\lambda $, and their sign is not the same in every model. The left block gives the share of agent-games matched exactly by one of the four canonical rules, below and above the sub-unit boundary, and the coefficient of a logistic regression of that indicator on $\log _{10}\lambda $. The right block gives the mean number of rounds that violate the nearest canonical rule at the smallest and largest scale, and the same trend fitted by least squares. Both use standard errors clustered on the dyad. Neither quantity depends on the learned classifier.}}{10}{table.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:strategy}{{3}{10}{\textbf {Rule agreement and rule distance against the payoff scale, by model.} Both $\beta $ columns are per decade of $\lam $, and their sign is not the same in every model. The left block gives the share of agent-games matched exactly by one of the four canonical rules, below and above the sub-unit boundary, and the coefficient of a logistic regression of that indicator on $\log _{10}\lam $. The right block gives the mean number of rounds that violate the nearest canonical rule at the smallest and largest scale, and the same trend fitted by least squares. Both use standard errors clustered on the dyad. Neither quantity depends on the learned classifier}{table.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Cooperation against the payoff scale, by model and prompt language.} One panel per model, one curve per prompt language. The annotation in each panel gives the smallest and largest across-scale range among that model's five languages. Each point averages 40 dyads, pooling the four persona pairings and the ten replicates.}}{10}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:language}{{2}{10}{\textbf {Cooperation against the payoff scale, by model and prompt language.} One panel per model, one curve per prompt language. The annotation in each panel gives the smallest and largest across-scale range among that model's five languages. Each point averages 40 dyads, pooling the four persona pairings and the ten replicates}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The persona effect changes with the payoff scale.} (a) Persona effect, the cooperation rate under a cooperative persona minus that under a selfish one, against the payoff scale; each point averages 200 dyads. The shaded band marks the two scales at which every payoff printed is at most 1; the effect changes sign across it in two models of five (table~\ref {tab:persona}). (b) The same contrast summarized: open markers give the mean persona effect at $\lambda \leq 0.1$, filled markers at $\lambda \geq 0.25$ (table~\ref {tab:persona}).}}{11}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:persona}{{3}{11}{\textbf {The persona effect changes with the payoff scale.} (a) Persona effect, the cooperation rate under a cooperative persona minus that under a selfish one, against the payoff scale; each point averages 200 dyads. The shaded band marks the two scales at which every payoff printed is at most 1; the effect changes sign across it in two models of five (table~\ref {tab:persona}). (b) The same contrast summarized: open markers give the mean persona effect at $\lam \leq 0.1$, filled markers at $\lam \geq 0.25$ (table~\ref {tab:persona})}{figure.caption.6}{}}
